  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "Johan Tarčulovski bears command responsibility for the killings, cruel treatment and destruction of homes because he exercised effective control over the police units that committed those acts and knew or had reason to know of them without taking necessary preventive or punitive measures.",
      "reasoning": "Customary international humanitarian law and the Rome Statute impose liability on superiors who have effective control over subordinates and who either order, facilitate, or fail to prevent or punish the commission of war crimes or crimes against humanity. The facts show that Tarčulovski was the officer who commanded the reserve police and private security forces entering Ljuboten, was present during the operation, and directed actions such as setting houses on fire and ordering the detention and beating of civilians. His position as a junior officer does not preclude effective control when he was the on‑site commander. Accordingly, the elements of command responsibility are satisfied.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The acts of shooting unarmed civilians, beating detainees, and deliberately burning civilian homes constitute war crimes of murder, torture and wanton destruction of property under the law of armed conflict, regardless of whether the operation was framed as a police action.",
      "reasoning": "The core principles of distinction and proportionality, which are peremptory norms of IHL, prohibit attacks against persons taking no active part in hostilities and the intentional targeting of civilian property not justified by military necessity. The evidence indicates that the victims were unarmed, wore civilian clothing, and were sheltering in basements; yet they were shot, beaten, and their homes were set ablaze. These facts demonstrate a deliberate attack on protected persons and objects, satisfying the substantive elements of war crimes as defined in customary IHL and the Rome Statute.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "An international armed conflict existed in Macedonia at the time of the Ljuboten operation, thereby triggering the application of IHL and rendering the conduct of the police units subject to the war‑crime regime.",
      "reasoning": "The threshold for an internal armed conflict is met when there is a protracted armed confrontation between governmental forces and organized armed groups, with a level of intensity and organization sufficient to exceed mere internal disturbances. The presence of the National Liberation Army, the prior land‑mine attack that killed eight soldiers, the deployment of reserve police and army mortar support, and the organized nature of the operation (use of an armoured personnel carrier, coordinated fire support, and systematic house‑burning) satisfy the criteria of intensity and organization recognized in customary IHL. Consequently, the conflict is classified as non‑international, and the conduct of the police is governed by the Geneva Conventions and Additional Protocol II, making the described acts war crimes.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "Even if the existence of an armed conflict were ultimately rejected, the conduct still qualifies as crimes against humanity because the acts were part of a widespread or systematic attack directed against a civilian population.",
      "reasoning": "The Rome Statute defines crimes against humanity to include murder, torture, and persecution when committed as part of a widespread or systematic attack against civilians. The operation in Ljuboten involved multiple coordinated acts—shootings, beatings, detentions, and arson—targeting the civilian population of the village, indicating a systematic pattern rather than isolated incidents. Therefore, irrespective of the conflict classification, the perpetrator can be held liable under the crimes‑against‑humanity framework.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
}
